To tell the Type System in TypeScript that a value may have multiple theoretical types but will only have a specific type in practice, you can use a technique called "type narrowing" or "type assertion." Here's a step-by-step process to achieve this:

1. Define the possible multiple types of the value:
   Let's assume you have a function that accepts an input of either string or number type:
   ```typescript
   function myFunction(input: string | number) {
     // Function body
   }
   ```
2. Use a conditional expression or a control flow statement to narrow the type:
   To assert that the input will only have one type, you can use a condition to evaluate the value of `input` and determine its type:
   ```typescript
   function myFunction(input: string | number) {
     if (typeof input === 'string') {
       // The input will only be a string here
       const myValue: string = input; // Narrowed type assertion

       // strings-specific processing goes here
     } else if (typeof input === 'number') {
       // The input will only be a number here
       const myValue: number = input; // Narrowed type assertion

       // numbers-specific processing goes here
     } else {
       // This should never be reached, as the input type will be either string or number
     }
   }
   ```
3. Utilize the narrowed type inside the corresponding block:
   Inside the respective if/else blocks, you can safely use the narrow type (string or number), without TypeScript complaining about the broader type (string | number). This allows you to write code that's specific to the known single type of the value.

   For example, in the "strings-specific processing" section, you can use string-specific functions and properties without worry, and the same goes for the "numbers-specific processing" section.

4. Optional: Assert types for other scenarios:
   If you have additional scenarios where you want to narrow the type further, you can add more if/else blocks or even use a switch statement, based on your specific requirements.

   Here's an extended example with a switch statement to illustrate this approach:
   ```typescript
   function myFunction(input: string | number) {
     switch (typeof input) {
       case 'string':
         const myValue: string = input; // Narrowed type assertion

         // strings-specific processing goes here
         break;
       case 'number':
         const myValue: number = input; // Narrowed type assertion

         // numbers-specific processing goes here
         break;
       default:
         throw new Error('Invalid input type. Expected string or number.');
     }
   }
   ```
   In this extended example, we've added a default case to handle any input types that don't match either 'string' or 'number'. This provides an extra layer of safety and validation.

By using type narrowing through conditional expressions or control flow statements, you can tell TypeScript that while the input value theoretically has multiple types (string or number), in practice, it will only have one type within each block, ensuring more accurate type checking and safer code.

